#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# kronecker_product

#% text
# `kronecker_product(a, b, ...)` gives the Kronecker product of matrices or vectors.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For an `m` by `n` matrix `a` and a `p` by `q` matrix `b`, the result is the `m*p` by `n*q` block matrix whose blocks are `a[i, j]*b`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
kronecker_product([[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[0, 1], [1, 0]])
#% output
#= [[0, 1, 0, 2], [1, 0, 2, 0], [0, 3, 0, 4], [3, 0, 4, 0]]

#% cell
kronecker_product([1, 2], [1, 10])
#% output
#= [1, 10, 2, 20]

#% cell
kronecker_product(identity_matrix(2), [[a, b], [c, d]])
#% output
#= [[a, b, 0, 0], [c, d, 0, 0], [0, 0, a, b], [0, 0, c, d]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The two-qubit state `|0> (x) |1>`:

#% cell
kronecker_product([1, 0], [0, 1])
#% output
#= [0, 1, 0, 0]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The mixed-product rule `(a (x) b).(c (x) d) = (a.c) (x) (b.d)`:

#% cell
a = [[1, 2], [0, 1]]; b = [[2, 0], [1, 1]]; c = [[0, 1], [1, 0]]; dd = [[1, 1], [0, 2]]; dot(kronecker_product(a, b), kronecker_product(c, dd)) == kronecker_product(dot(a, c), dot(b, dd))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The determinant:

#% cell
[det(kronecker_product(a, b)), det(a)^2*det(b)^2]
#% output
#= [4, 4]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `dot`, `outer`, `identity_matrix`
